    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mother 3 is a Japanese role-playing video game written by Shigesato Itoi and published by Nintendo for the Game Boy Advance in 2006. Although it was only released in Japan, many fans abroad wished to play the game, so much so that a team led by a professional Japanese-to-English translator named Clyde Mandelin spent two years translating the game into English. This thesis explores why Mother 3 is so beloved—its unique narrative depth, its anticapitalist messaging, and its metaleptic humor. In the first chapter of this thesis, I investigate the ways in which the video game functions as a form of storytelling, developing a framework through which to conceptualize space and time in the video game. In the second chapter of this thesis, I examine Mother 3 chapter by chapter, narrating significant events in the order of their occurrence and analyzing the various dimensions of which these events are composed. Itoi, I eventually argue, offers as a remedy for the evils of capitalism a worldview that emphasizes nonattachment and the honoring of memory. Mother 3 is at its core a celebration of life, transience, and every moment that comprises existence, and it encourages its players to immortalize its world by cherishing every experience it has to offer—including the strange, the funny, and the heartrending.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Xiong, J. (2024). Sunflowers and Illusions: Mother 3 and the Video Game as a Form of Storytelling. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/fm703kj1299. https://doi.org/10.25740/fm703kj1299.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
